<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Allocation Problem</div>
<h2>Where Should Labeled Data Go?</h2>
<p>
  LLM post-training has a scarce resource: labeled verifiable examples. Each one needs a problem with a checkable answer and a reliable grader. The standard recipe spends every labeled example on the deployment model—if the target is a 1.7B student, run GRPO on it directly.
</p>
<p>
  This paper asks whether that's the right allocation. The answer is no—and the intuition is straightforward: sparse reward is useful only where exploration is productive. A 1.7B model solving AIME 2024 problems has near-zero initial pass rate. GRPO's rollouts are almost all failures. There's no gradient signal to exploit because the model can barely find any correct trajectories to reinforce.
</p>
<p>
  The 8B and 14B models are in a qualitatively different position. They solve many more problems correctly, so their rollouts contain genuine signal. The same labeled data produces a much better reward-shaped distribution when applied to them.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">direct GRPO performance by model size — same algorithm, same data</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">scroll into view to animate</span>
