<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-signals" data-code="signals">
<div class="section-no">02 — Sparse vs Dense Signals</div>
<h2>Two Kinds of Learning Signal</h2>
<p>
  Post-training methods differ in the density of the reward signal they provide. Understanding the distinction is key to the whole paper.
</p>
<p>
  A <strong>sparse reward</strong> is sequence-level: you generate a complete response, then get a single binary score — correct or incorrect. That's one learning signal per trajectory, no matter how many tokens it contains. For a 500-token AIME solution, sparse RL delivers one bit of feedback.
</p>
<p>
  A <strong>dense reward</strong> is token-level: at every position in the response, you receive a quality score. If a teacher model has already solved the problem correctly, its log-probabilities at each token represent a dense, per-step signal about how good that token choice was. For the same 500-token solution, dense supervision delivers 500 gradient targets.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">sparse vs dense signal — walk through an example reasoning step</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Prev / Next</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="signals-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num" id="sig-num-0">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">The problem</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Model receives: <em>"Find all integer solutions to x² − 5x + 6 = 0."</em> It generates a 120-token solution. With sparse reward, nothing happens until the answer is submitted.</div>
          <div class="step-code">prompt → [generate 120 tokens] → submit answer</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="sig-num-1">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Sparse: one signal at the end</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Verifier checks final answer. Student wrote "x = 2 or x = 3" — correct! Score = +1. Or wrote "x = 1 or x = 6" — wrong! Score = 0. Either way, only one learning signal for all 120 tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">R(x, y) = 1[final_answer_correct]  # one bit per trajectory</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="sig-num-2">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Dense: teacher logprobs at every token</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Teacher (RL-trained 8B) is queried at each of the student's 120 token positions. At token 14 ("factor"), teacher assigns high probability. At token 47 ("add"), teacher assigns low probability — student is drifting off track. Per-token gradient at every step.</div>
          <div class="step-code">r_T(s_t, y_t) = β · log π_T(y_t | s_t)  # per-token implicit reward</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="sig-num-3">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Why density matters at cold start</div>
          <div class="step-desc">A 1.7B student at cold start solves &lt;20% of AIME problems. With sparse reward, 80%+ of rollouts contribute zero gradient. Dense teacher supervision provides gradient even on failed trajectories — the teacher's logprobs signal which early tokens were already going wrong.</div>
          <div class="step-code">sparse: 80% of rollouts → zero gradient
dense:  100% of rollouts → gradient signal</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="sig-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="sig-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="sig-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The paper frames this as an <em>allocation</em> problem. Dense teacher supervision requires a teacher that is itself reward-shaped — a raw pre-trained teacher has high-entropy logprobs that don't represent correct reasoning chains. The dense signal is only as good as its source.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-workflow" data-code="workflow">
<div class="section-no">03 — The 4-Stage Workflow</div>
<h2>Teacher First, Student Second</h2>
<p>
  The reward-density principle yields a four-stage pipeline. The same pool of labeled training data powers all four stages, but different models receive different signal densities at different times.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">four-stage workflow — labeled data allocation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Next to walk through</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="workflow-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num" id="wf-num-0">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 1 — Teacher RL (sparse reward on teacher)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run GRPO on the 8B teacher using the full labeled data pool D. Teacher explores with high-quality rollouts, concentrating its distribution on correct reasoning chains. Output: reward-shaped teacher π_T that approximates the KL-regularized optimal policy.</div>
          <div class="step-code">teacher_rl(π_T, D)  # GRPO with verifier reward R(x, y)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="wf-num-1">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 2a — Forward-KL Warmup (off-policy dense)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Sample K rollouts from the RL-shaped teacher. Train the 1.7B student to match the teacher's next-token distribution on those rollouts. This is supervised training under teacher occupancy — mode-covering and well-conditioned because it runs on teacher-supported tokens.</div>
          <div class="step-code">L_F(θ) = E_&#123;s~d_π_T&#125; KL(π_T(·|s) ‖ π_θ(·|s))
# student trains on teacher rollouts (off-policy)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="wf-num-2">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 2b — On-Policy Distillation / OPD (on-policy dense)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Now sample rollouts from the warmed-up student. Query the frozen teacher for logprobs at each student token position. Minimize reverse-KL under student occupancy. The student gets corrected on its own failure modes — token positions it visits but the teacher considers wrong.</div>
          <div class="step-code">L_R(θ) = E_&#123;s~d_π_θ&#125; KL(π_θ(·|s) ‖ π_T(·|s))
# student rollouts, teacher scores (on-policy)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num" id="wf-num-3">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Stage 3 — Post-Bridge Student RL (optional)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">If any labeled data was held out from Stages 1–2, run GRPO on the now-trained student. The bridge changed student trainability: sparse reward that was nearly useless on a cold 1.7B now gives a +3.1 MATH-point lift. Only makes sense after the bridge has warmed the student up.</div>
          <div class="step-code">student_rl(π_θ_bridged, D_held_out)  # sparse on warm student</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="wf-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="wf-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="wf-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The two-stage bridge (2a + 2b) handles a specific problem: coverage mismatch. A cold 1.7B student has very different token distributions than a post-RL 8B teacher. If you run OPD directly without warmup, the per-token implicit reward has extremely high variance — the teacher assigns near-zero probability to tokens the student commonly generates, producing unstable gradient updates. Stage 2a closes that gap first.
</p>

<div class="callout c-cyan">
  <span class="callout-icon">🔗</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    <strong>Relation to existing methods:</strong> DeepSeek-R1 distillation = Stage 1 + off-policy half of Stage 2a only. Standalone OPD = Stage 2b alone. Standard SFT-then-RL = skip Stages 1–2 entirely. Each omission costs points, as the ablation shows.
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-math" data-code="math">
<div class="section-no">04 — OPD as Implicit RL</div>
<h2>The Math: On-Policy Distillation is Trust-Region RL</h2>
<p>
  The paper provides a clean theoretical grounding for why the workflow's stage order matters. The key insight: each OPD step is mathematically equivalent to a trust-region RL update with a dense teacher-induced implicit reward.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">OPD Gradient = Dense Implicit RL (Eq. 3)</div>
  −β ∇<sub>θ</sub> KL(π<sub>θ</sub> ‖ π<sub>T</sub>)|<sub>θ_k</sub>
  = E<sub>y~π_k</sub> [ <span class="math-var">R̃_k</span>(x,y) · ∇<sub>θ</sub> log π<sub>θ</sub>(y|x) ]
  <br>
  where &nbsp;<span class="math-var">R̃_k</span>(x,y) = Σ<sub>t</sub> β log [ π<sub>T</sub>(y_t|s_t) / π_k(y_t|s_t) ]
  <div class="math-comment">  ← per-token log-ratio: how much teacher prefers this token over current student</div>
</div>

<p>
  Each OPD gradient step equals the policy gradient of a dense per-token reward: the teacher's log-probability relative to the current student. This is exactly a local trust-region update maximizing expected implicit reward minus a KL penalty to the current student.
</p>
<p>
  This framing reveals two necessary conditions for OPD to be informative:
</p>

<div class="callout c-amber">
  <span class="callout-icon">⚠</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    <strong>Condition C1 — Teacher must be reward-shaped.</strong> If π<sub>T</sub> has not been trained by sparse reward, the implicit reward R̃ pushes the student toward an unshaped distribution — larger, but not correct-answer-aware. The signal is wrong, not just noisy. This is why transferring a raw teacher (no Stage 1) is <em>worse</em> than cold GRPO: you're doing RL toward the wrong target.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout c-amber">
  <span class="callout-icon">⚠</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    <strong>Condition C2 — Teacher must be close to student.</strong> When teacher and student have little coverage overlap, the per-token ratio log(π<sub>T</sub>/π_k) is high-variance: large magnitudes on tokens the teacher likes but the student rarely generates. OPD gradients become dominated by a few outlier token positions, making updates unstable. Stage 2a's forward-KL warmup brings the student onto teacher-supported tokens, satisfying C2 before OPD begins.
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The theory makes three falsifiable predictions, all confirmed by the ablation:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>P1:</strong> Remove Stage 1 (raw teacher) → bridge endpoint falls below direct student GRPO. Raw teacher violates C1.</li>
  <li><strong>P2:</strong> Remove Stage 2a (OPD-only, no warmup) → endpoint drops. Cold student violates C2.</li>
  <li><strong>P3:</strong> Add Stage 3 after bridge → sparse reward now lifts the student. Bridge changes trainability.</li>
</ul>
</section>

