  The same three-way ordering reproduces. A 9× larger raw teacher is still worse than cold GRPO on the 8B student (55.4% vs 59.8% MATH). The same teacher after Stage 1 RL outperforms both. Scale does not satisfy Condition C1 — reward-shaping does.
</p>
<p>
  The absolute gains are smaller in the Llama experiment (+2.3 MATH points vs +3.4 for Qwen3-1.7B). The paper attributes this to the 8B Llama student having higher base capacity than the 1.7B Qwen3 student — less recoverable headroom from the allocation shift.
</p>

<h3>Operational Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Don't spend labeled post-training data on the deployment model first. Allocate it to the strongest available teacher.</li>
  <li>Teacher scale alone doesn't help — the teacher must be RL-trained on the verifier. A raw large teacher distilled densely is <em>actively harmful.</em></li>
  <li>The FKL warmup before OPD is not optional. Coverage mismatch between a cold small student and a post-RL large teacher destabilizes OPD gradients.</li>
  <li>After the bridge, sparse reward on the student works. Reserve held-out labeled data for a post-bridge student RL stage.</li>
  <li>The recipe requires a shared tokenizer between teacher and student — this is the main constraint on which teacher-student pairs can use OPD.</li>
</ul>
</section>

